  "content": {
    "id": "p60-task-17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $y=x^2+4x+2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p60-task-17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the $x$-intercepts of the graph.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-2-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(-2+\\sqrt{2},0)$.",
          "worked": "At the $x$-intercepts, $y=0$. Using the quadratic formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+4x+2&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{-4\\pm\\sqrt{4^2-4(1)(2)}}{2(1)}\\\\x&=-2\\pm\\sqrt{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe intercepts are $(-2-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(-2+\\sqrt{2},0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p60-task-17-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of $y=x^2+4x+2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Upward-opening parabola with vertex $(-2,-2)$, $x$-intercepts $(-2\\pm\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $y$-intercept $(0,2)$.",
          "worked": "The positive coefficient of $x^2$ gives an upward-opening parabola. The vertex lies halfway between the roots:\n$$\\begin{align*}x_{\\mathrm v}&=\\frac{(-2-\\sqrt{2})+(-2+\\sqrt{2})}{2}\\\\x_{\\mathrm v}&=-2\\\\y_{\\mathrm v}&=(-2)^2+4(-2)+2\\\\y_{\\mathrm v}&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=0$, $y=2$. Sketch the parabola through both intercepts from part (a), with vertex $(-2,-2)$ and $y$-intercept $(0,2)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p60-task-17-b-solution-graph",
              "role": "solution",
